{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '621,677p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; cat test/yang/antlr/conformance.cljc; rg -n 'mutation|canonical|synthetic|4 99|conform|error.*coordinate' collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md; ls /opt/homebrew/bin/git /usr/local/bin/git\"","aggregated_output":"### 4.6 The multi-target pipeline and the parse-tree IR\n\nThe multi-target design (2026-10-17; approved by a codex adversarial\nreview after two revision rounds) makes the per-host generation of\nsection 4.2 concrete for the Python frontend and generalizes the\nrecipe to every future ANTLR-supported language.\n\nThe manifest gains a `:targets` map: one entry per host target (Java,\nJavaScript, Dart), each carrying its generation flags, helper bases and\npatches, digests, and output directory. The generator loops the map;\nthe Java digests stay byte-identical to today. The runtimes pin per\ntarget (the Maven runtime, the antlr4 npm package, the antlr4 pub\npackage, all at the tool version).\n\nThe target-neutral parse-tree IR is the existing CST packet v1,\nformalized. ANTLR has no target-neutral tree, and the per-target parse\ntrees differ in their objects, but for the same grammar their structure\nis equivalent. The design names the CST packet the IR: each host's\nexporter translates its target's parse tree into the same packet\nvalues, the packet's `validate!` enforces the seven invariants with a\none-pass recursive preorder walk and outcome-specific contracts\n(duplicate edges, DFS preorder, real-token text against source bytes,\nUTF-8 boundary alignment, terminal ordering, rule extents over real\ndescendants, grammar-metadata name checks, and error packets as their\nown outcome), and a portable `conform` is the per-target oracle. A\n`:synthetic-policy` map keys synthetic justification on grammar role\n(DEDENT always, NEWLINE at end, EOF always, other types never), and\nerror packets carry two independent coordinate systems: the byte span\ncomputed from token indexes, and ANTLR's line and column captured\nverbatim, which are not required to agree.\n\nThe lowering stays per language by design and consumes the IR\nhost-neutrally; it never sees a target runtime. Per language, the whole\nper-host surface is one small parser adapter with three\nreader-conditional constructor profiles.\n\nAdding a new ANTLR-supported language becomes a bounded recipe: vendor\nthe grammars, add the manifest's target entries, generate, write the\nsmall parser adapter and the language's lowering and prelude, add the\nconformance goldens. The Python multi-host work is the first instance of\nthis recipe, not a special case.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Source ingestion\n\n### 5.1 Source units are explicit\n\nSource is read via `dao.stream`. Use a versioned source vocabulary with\nevents such as:\n\n```clojure\n{:yang.source/event :yang.source/chunk\n :yang.source/unit [\"session-token\" 7]\n :yang.source/revision 3\n :yang.source/ordinal 0\n :yang.source/text \"def f(x):\\n\"}\n(ns yang.antlr.conformance\n  \"The per-target, language-free exporter oracle (docs/design/yang.antlr.md\n   §5.5): a language's fixtures are run against the exporter of the running\n   host, JVM, Node or Dart, and every one must come out the same. A fixture is\n   a source string and either its expected `shape` or the error it must\n   produce. The goldens are the JVM's; they are the cross-target oracle.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.test :refer [is testing]]\n    [yang.antlr.packet :as packet]))\n\n\n(defn shape\n  \"A packet's nodes as [rule span] and [type text span] entries in preorder.\"\n  [pk]\n  (mapv (fn [n]\n          (if (= :rule (:kind n))\n            [(:rule n) (:span n)]\n            [(:type n) (:text n) (:span n)]))\n        (:yang.cst/nodes pk)))\n\n\n(defn check-ok\n  \"`source` parses to an ok packet that validates and conforms (invariants\n   1-5); with `expected` the packet's shape equals it. Returns the packet.\"\n  ([parse source] (check-ok parse source nil))\n  ([parse source expected]\n   (let [pk (parse source)]\n     (is (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome pk)) source)\n     (is (some? (packet/validate! pk)) source)\n     (is (= [] (packet/conform pk source)) source)\n     (when expected\n       (is (= expected (shape pk)) source))\n     pk)))\n\n\n(defn check-error\n  \"`source` yields a syntax-error packet with no nodes whose first error has\n   the given `line` and `span` (invariant 6). Returns the packet.\"\n  [parse source {:keys [line span]}]\n  (let [pk (parse source)\n        e (first (:yang.cst/errors pk))]\n    (testing source\n      (is (= :yang.cst/syntax-error (:yang.cst/outcome pk)))\n      (is (not (contains? pk :yang.cst/nodes)) \"never a partial program\")\n      (is (not (contains? pk :yang.cst/root)))\n      (is (true? (:yang.cst/complete pk)))\n      (is (= :syntax-error (:kind e)))\n      (is (string? (:message e)))\n      (when line (is (= line (:line e))))\n      (when span (is (= span (:span e)))))\n    pk))\n\n\n(defn check-fixtures\n  \"Run each fixture {:source s :shape [...]} or {:source s :error {...}}.\"\n  [parse fixtures]\n  (doseq [{:keys [source shape error]} fixtures]\n    (if error\n      (check-error parse source error)\n      (check-ok parse source shape))))\n43:  :type :text :span :synthetic` through `packet/*`. Hand-built packets already\n64:conformance fixtures. Nothing per language touches the generator, the exporter,\n91:  4. A token is `:synthetic true` with a zero-width span when its text is not\n99:- **Add** a portable `packet/conform` (name final; the engineer picks) that\n101:  monotonicity in preorder, byte-span inside `[0, utf8-length]`, synthetic\n103:  conformance oracle (section 5) and costs nothing at runtime (tests only).\n236:1. **Exporter conformance (per target, language-free)**: a new cljc\n237:   `yang.antlr.conformance` test helper takes a profile and a fixture list\n239:   `validate!` and `conform` pass; the `shape` (rule/type/text/span) equals the\n242:   no nodes; EOF/DEDENT synthetic and zero-width. `cst_export_test.clj` becomes\n293:5. Conformance fixtures: `[source shape]` goldens for `yang.antlr.conformance`;\n311:  `yang.antlr.cst` files, `yang.antlr.conformance`, `yang.frontend`.\n326:  formalization and `conform`; the conformance helper; the probe split; the\n332:  `packet.cljc` too (the doc move and `conform`). Whichever lands second\n350:| M1 IR + exporters | `packet.cljc` owns the v1 shape doc and `conform`; `cst.cljs`, `cst.cljd`; shared `parse-transform`; `yang.antlr.conformance`; `cst_export_test` → cljc | conformance goldens green on JVM, Node, Dart including the UTF-8 span fixture and error packets; `conform` passes on every golden and on every precompiled packet |\n387:   a time; confirm `Testing yang.antlr.conformance-test` and\nls: /opt/homebrew/bin/git: No such file or directory\nls: /usr/local/bin/git: No such file or directory\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
