24\t  (let [pk (parser/parse-source \"x = $\\n\")]\n   125\t    (is (= :yang.cst/syntax-error (:yang.cst/outcome pk)))\n   126\t    (is (= [4 5] (:span (first (:yang.cst/errors pk)))))))\n   127\t\n   128\t\n   129\t(deftest exporter-is-deterministic-test\n   130\t  (let [src \"def f(a):\\n    return [a, {1: 'x'}]\\n\"]\n   131\t    (is (= (parser/parse-source src)\n   132\t           (parser/parse-source (parser/make-worker) [:unit 0] src)))))\n   133\t\n   134\t\n   135\t(deftest every-grammar-rule-name-is-exported-by-name-test\n   136\t  (testing \"rule records use the grammar's rule names\"\n   137\t    (let [names (set parser/rule-names)\n   138\t          pk (parser/parse-source\n   139\t               \"class A(B):\\n    def m(self, *a):\\n        while x: pass\\n\")]\n   140\t      (is (every? #(contains? names (:rule %))\n   141\t                  (filter #(= :rule (:kind %)) (:yang.cst/nodes pk)))))))\n   142\t\n   143\t\n   144\t;; =============================================================================\n   145\t;; The stage: source events in, packets out\n   633\tpackage, all at the tool version).\n   634\t\n   635\tThe target-neutral parse-tree IR is the existing CST packet v1,\n   636\tformalized. ANTLR has no target-neutral tree, and the per-target parse\n   637\ttrees differ in their objects, but for the same grammar their structure\n   638\tis equivalent. The design names the CST packet the IR: each host's\n   639\texporter translates its target's parse tree into the same packet\n   640\tvalues, the packet's `validate!` enforces the seven invariants with a\n   641\tone-pass recursive preorder walk and outcome-specific contracts\n   642\t(duplicate edges, DFS preorder, real-token text against source bytes,\n   643\tUTF-8 boundary alignment, terminal ordering, rule extents over real\n   644\tdescendants, grammar-metadata name checks, and error packets as their\n   645\town outcome), and a portable `conform` is the per-target oracle. A\n   646\t`:synthetic-policy` map keys synthetic justification on grammar role\n   647\t(DEDENT always, NEWLINE at end, EOF always, other types never), and\n   648\terror packets carry two independent coordinate systems: the byte span\n   649\tcomputed from token indexes, and ANTLR's line and column captured\n   650\tverbatim, which are not required to agree.\n   651\t\n   652\tThe lowering stays per language by design and consumes the IR\n   653\thost-neutrally; it never sees a target runtime. Per language, the whole\n   654\tper-host surface is one small parser adapter with three\n?? collab/1792282000000-engineer-antlr-multi-target.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792289000000-architect-antlr-multi-plan.claude-cloud-fable.findings.md\n?? collab/1792291000000-engineer-antlr-multi-m012.claude-sonnet-5-5.report.md\n?? collab/1792291000000-engineer-antlr-multi-m012.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792304000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-plan-r3.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n?? collab/1792340000000-engineer-antlr-multi-m012c.prompt.md\n?? collab/1792360000000-reviewer-antlr-multi-m012-gate.prompt.md\n?? collab/m012b-report.md\n?? collab/m012c-report.log\n?? collab/m012c-smoke-in.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
